  The connector Fuseki 6.2.0 builds for itself (JettyServer.jettyServer and
  JettyLib.httpConfiguration: 2 to 20 threads, port 3030, 512 KiB request headers, 16 KiB
  response headers, no Server header), with one difference: the output buffer.

  Fuseki sets it to 5 MiB. Jetty takes that buffer from its pool for every response that has a
  body, the pool keeps no buffer above 64 KiB, and so each response allocates 5 MiB of direct
  memory that only a collection frees. Under query load that fills -XX:MaxDirectMemorySize
  several times a second, and each time the JVM asks for a collection. At 32 KiB, Jetty's own
  default, the pool reuses the buffer. A result then reaches the client 32 KiB at a time while
  the query runs. Fuseki sends results chunked with either buffer.

  The entrypoint passes this file as fuseki-server's Jetty configuration. The DOCTYPE is needed:
  Jetty validates the file, against a DTD it carries in its own jar.
-->
<Configure id="Server" class="org.eclipse.jetty.server.Server">
  <Get name="threadPool">
    <Set name="minThreads" type="int">2</Set>
    <Set name="maxThreads" type="int">20</Set>
  </Get>
  <Call name="addConnector">
    <Arg>
      <New class="org.eclipse.jetty.server.ServerConnector">
        <Arg name="server"><Ref refid="Server"/></Arg>
        <Arg name="factories">
          <Array type="org.eclipse.jetty.server.ConnectionFactory">
            <Item>
              <New class="org.eclipse.jetty.server.HttpConnectionFactory">
                <Arg>
                  <New class="org.eclipse.jetty.server.HttpConfiguration">
                    <Set name="requestHeaderSize" type="int">524288</Set>
                    <Set name="responseHeaderSize" type="int">16384</Set>
                    <Set name="outputBufferSize" type="int">32768</Set>
                    <Set name="sendServerVersion" type="boolean">false</Set>
                  </New>
                </Arg>
              </New>
            </Item>
          </Array>
        </Arg>
        <Set name="port" type="int">3030</Set>
      </New>
    </Arg>
  </Call>
</Configure>
